  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is <b>AWS CodeDeploy supports automatic rollback based on CloudWatch alarm breaches.</b> You can enable automatic rollback to trigger whenever a deployment fails or a configured alarm (for example, an Amazon CloudWatch Alarm) goes into an alarm state. This reduces the risk of prolonged outages in production environments by reverting to the previous working version automatically.</p><p>Option <i>You can configure SNS alerts for deployment events, but initiating a rollback through SNS notifications is not supported.</i> is incorrect because while SNS notifications can inform you of deployment results, they cannot independently trigger a rollback.</p><p>Option <i>There is no out-of-the-box automatic rollback for a deployment group; rollbacks must be started manually every time.</i> is incorrect as AWS CodeDeploy has built-in features for automatic rollback under certain conditions, such as deployment failure or CloudWatch alarm breaches.</p><p>Option <i>Automatic rollback is exclusive to EC2 deployments and cannot be set up for on-premises environments.</i> is also incorrect because CodeDeploy allows automatic rollback for both EC2/On-Premises deployment types.</p><p><b>Tip:</b> Always link your deployments to suitable CloudWatch alarms for critical production workloads, and enable rollback actions to minimize the impact of failed deployments.</p>",
  "query": "<p>Your organization has built a new AWS CodeDeploy application to streamline deployments on Amazon EC2 instances within an Auto Scaling group. Suppose a deployment attempt fails because of faulty software. Your supervisor requests that you set up automated rollback for the production deployment group.<br>Which statement about configuring automatic rollback for AWS CodeDeploy is accurate?</p>",
